## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by reference — a new reference on every render means a new prop, even if the data is identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a new object/function reference
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />        // new {} each time
<Child onClick={() => handleClick()} />   // new () => each time
```

React re-renders a child whenever its parent renders, unless you opt out with `React.memo` — but even then, unstable references defeat the optimization.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Hook/API | What it memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A component's output | Child is pure and renders often with same props |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Expensive calculation; or stabilizing an object reference passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Stabilizing a callback passed to a memoized child |

**Rules of thumb:**
- Don't reach for these by default — profiling first.
- `useCallback` is only useful if the receiving component is wrapped in `React.memo`.
- `useMemo` for values is worth it when the computation is measurably expensive (not for trivial objects).

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value re-creates on every render of its provider unless memoized. Every consumer re-renders when the context value changes by reference.

```jsx
// Bad: new object every render → all consumers re-render
<Ctx.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>

// Fix: stabilize the value
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<Ctx.Provider value={value}>
```

Also consider **splitting contexts** — a `UserContext` and a `AuthActionsContext` — so components that only need actions don't re-render when user data changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders every time `App` renders, even when `items` hasn't changed, because `handleSelect` is a new function each render.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const items = ['a', 'b', 'c'];

  // New function reference on every App render
  const handleSelect = (item) => setSelected(item);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <List items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = ({ items, onSelect }) => {
  console.log('List rendered');
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
};
```

Typing in the input re-renders `App` → re-renders `List`, even though `items` and `onSelect` logic didn't change.

**After** — `List` only re-renders when `items` or `onSelect` actually changes.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const items = ['a', 'b', 'c']; // stable (defined outside or useMemo'd)

  // Stable reference — only changes if setSelected changes (it never does)
  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => setSelected(item), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <List items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(({ items, onSelect }) => {
  console.log('List rendered');
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
});
```

`useCallback` stabilizes `handleSelect`; `React.memo` makes `List` skip renders when props haven't changed. Typing now only re-renders `App` — `List` is skipped.

---

**Key takeaway:** memoization only helps when (a) the component does meaningful work and (b) its props are actually stable. Profile with React DevTools Profiler before optimizing.
